{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: forecasts are systematically too high versus realized prices, especially beyond ~day 7–10. Apply a downward level shift that grows with horizon (small in week 1, larger in weeks 2–4).",
    "Downtrend underreaction: when the recent 30d history is falling, the model continues to project stabilization/rebound too early. Add a trend-following correction (more negative drift) for the first ~2 weeks after a clear downtrend regime.",
    "Rebound/mean-reversion overshoot: the model repeatedly predicts stronger mean reversion (up-moves) than occurs; realized path keeps drifting lower. Damp positive day-to-day changes and cap rebounds after local lows.",
    "Late-horizon dispersion too wide: weeks 3–4 forecasts sit well above outcomes and fluctuate upward; compress the forecast path toward a lower trajectory (shrink deviations from a downward-sloping baseline).",
    "Turning-point lag: after sharp declines, the model’s implied bottom occurs later/higher than reality. Shift inflection points earlier and at lower levels (i.e., accelerate the decline and reduce the subsequent bounce)."
  ]
}
